
{"id":"9d9cfd64-a41a-44c0-8d5d-c933ca2e630b","object":"chat.completion","created":1784822784,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import json\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    min_cluster_size = payload['min_cluster_size']\\n    clusters = payload['clusters']\\n    parent = payload['parent']\\n\\n    # Validation\\n    if not isinstance(min_cluster_size, (int, float)) or min_cluster_size < 0:\\n        raise ValueError('min_cluster_size must be a non-negative number')\\n    if not isinstance(clusters, list) or not clusters:\\n        raise ValueError('clusters must be a non-empty list')\\n    if not isinstance(parent, dict):\\n        raise ValueError('parent must be a dict')\\n\\n    cluster_map = {}\\n    for c in clusters:\\n        if not isinstance(c, dict):\\n            raise ValueError('each cluster must be a dict')\\n        cid = c.get('cluster_id')\\n        if not isinstance(cid, str):\\n            raise ValueError('cluster_id must be a string')\\n        if cid in cluster_map:\\n            raise ValueError(f'duplicate cluster_id {cid}')\\n        size = c.get('size')\\n        if not isinstance(size, int) or size < 0:\\n            raise ValueError('size must be a non-negative integer')\\n        birth = c.get('birth_lambda')\\n        if not isinstance(birth, (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError('birth_lambda must be a number')\\n        exits = c.get('point_exit_lambdas')\\n        if not isinstance(exits, list) or len(exits) != size:\\n            raise ValueError('point_exit_lambdas must be a list of length equal to size')\\n        for e in exits:\\n            if not isinstance(e, (int, float)):\\n                raise ValueError('each exit lambda must be a number')\\n        cluster_map[cid] = {'size': size, 'birth': birth, 'exits': exits}\\n\\n    for cid, p in parent.items():\\n        if cid not in cluster_map:\\n            raise ValueError(f'parent key {cid} not in clusters')\\n        if p is not None and p not in cluster_map:\\n            raise ValueError(f'parent value {p} not in clusters')\\n\\n    # Filter retained clusters\\n    retained_ids = set()\\n    retained_list = []\\n    for c in clusters:\\n        if c['size'] >= min_cluster_size:\\n            retained_ids.add(c['cluster_id'])\\n            retained_list.append(c)\\n\\n    # Compute stability\\n    stability = {}\\n    for c in retained_list:\\n        cid = c['cluster_id']\\n        birth = c['birth_lambda']\\n        total = 0.0\\n        for e in c['point_exit_lambdas']:\\n            total += max(0.0, e - birth)\\n        stability[cid] = total\\n\\n    # Build tree of retained clusters\\n    node_parent = {}\\n    node_children = {}\\n    for cid in retained_ids:\\n        node_parent[cid] = None\\n        node_children[cid] = []\\n\\n    for cid in retained_ids:\\n        p = parent[cid]\\n        while p is not None and p not in retained_ids:\\n            p = parent.get(p)\\n        node_parent[cid] = p\\n        if p is not None:\\n            node_children[p].append(cid)\\n\\n    # Compute ancestors and descendants for each node\\n    ancestors = {}\\n    descendants = {}\\n    # Helper to get all ancestors including self\\n    def get_ancestors(cid):\\n        res = [cid]\\n        p = node_parent[cid]\\n        while p is not None:\\n            res.append(p)\\n            p = node_parent[p]\\n        return res\\n    for cid in retained_ids:\\n        ancestors[cid] = get_ancestors(cid)\\n\\n    # Compute descendants via DFS from roots\\n    visited = set()\\n    def dfs(cid):\\n        visited.add(cid)\\n        desc = set()\\n        for child in node_children[cid]:\\n            if child not in visited:\\n                dfs(child)\\n            desc.add(child)\\n            desc.update(descendants[child])\\n        descendants[cid] = desc\\n    for cid in retained_ids:\\n        if node_parent[cid] is None:\\n            dfs(cid)\\n    # In case forest, ensure all visited\\n    for cid in retained_ids:\\n        if cid not in visited:\\n            dfs(cid)\\n\\n    # Sort by decreasing stability, then by cluster_id (ascending)\\n    sorted_clusters = sorted(retained_ids, key=lambda cid: (-stability[cid], cid))\\n\\n    selected = set()\\n    blocked = set()\\n    for cid in sorted_clusters:\\n        if cid in blocked:\\n            continue\\n        # Check descendants: any selected descendant\\n        if any(d in selected for d in descendants[cid]):\\n            continue\\n        # Check ancestors: any selected ancestor\\n        if any(a in selected for a in ancestors[cid] if a != cid):\\n            continue\\n        selected.add(cid)\\n        # Block ancestors (excluding self)\\n        for a in ancestors[cid]:\\n            if a != cid:\\n                blocked.add(a)\\n\\n    # Build result\\n    clusters_result = []\\n    for c in retained_list:\\n        cid = c['cluster_id']\\n        clusters_result.append({\\n            'cluster_id': cid,\\n            'stability': stability[cid]\\n        })\\n    clusters_result.sort(key=lambda x: x['cluster_id'])\\n    selected_sorted = sorted(selected)\\n    return {'clusters': clusters_result, 'selected': selected_sorted}\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to parse the problem: Implement `solve(case)` for \"condensed_tree_stability\". The input case has fields: min_cluster_size, clusters (list of dicts with cluster_id, size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas), parent (dict mapping cluster_id to parent cluster_id or null for root). The goal: condense hierarchy under min_cluster_size, compute stability for each retained cluster (sum of max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) over its points), rank by decreasing stability then by cluster ID (presumably ascending? \"cluster ID\" string, but typical tie-breaking: increasing cluster_id order? Problem says \"by decreasing stability and cluster ID\". For ties in stability, cluster ID determines order. Need to decide: ascending? Usually we sort by ( -stability, cluster_id ) with cluster_id ascending. But we need to \"select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" That's a typical HDBSCAN procedure: after computing stability, process clusters in order of decreasing stability, and for each, if its descendant clusters have not been selected, and it is not a descendant of an already selected cluster, then select it. Then block ancestors. Actually typical algorithm: start from leaves upwards, compute stability, then traverse clusters in decreasing stability, and select a cluster if it has no selected descendant (i.e., all its descendants are not selected), and if selected, then all its ancestors are blocked from being selected. So we need to implement condensed tree: For each cluster, we have size and birth_lambda and point_exit_lambdas. The cluster's points are the points that belong to that cluster at its birth. The cluster exists from birth_lambda until the minimum of point_exit_lambdas? Actually in HDBSCAN, each point has a lambda value at which it exits the cluster (when it becomes noise or moves to a parent? In the condensed tree, each leaf cluster corresponds to a point? Wait typical: The cluster hierarchy is built from the single-linkage tree, then condensed by removing clusters smaller than min_cluster_size. The condensed tree has clusters (with size >= min_cluster_size) and each cluster has a birth lambda (lambda at which it appears) and a set of points associated, each with an exit lambda (lambda at which it leaves the cluster, either by becoming noise or moving to a smaller cluster). The stability of a cluster is sum over its points of (exit_lambda - birth_lambda) for points with exit_lambda > birth_lambda, i.e., points that persist beyond birth. The problem gives for each cluster: size (number of points?), birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas (list of exit lambdas for its points, length = size?). So compute stability: for each exit lambda, compute max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda), sum. That is straightforward.\n\nNow the condensation: First drop clusters smaller than min_cluster_size. But we need to handle parent relationships: the given parent mapping is for all clusters (including those that might be dropped). After dropping small clusters, the condensed tree must be recomputed: clusters that were children of a dropped cluster become children of the dropped cluster's parent? Actually typical condensed tree: clusters with size < min_cluster_size are removed, and their points are assigned to the nearest ancestor cluster that remains. But the problem says \"Drop clusters smaller than the minimum cluster size.\" That likely means we simply ignore those clusters in the stability computation and selection. However, we need to consider that points from dropped clusters still belong to some ancestor. But the input provides point_exit_lambdas only for the clusters as given, not per point. So we must assume that the cluster's point_exit_lambdas are the exit lambdas for the points that are in that cluster (i.e., the points contained within it at its birth). When we drop a cluster, we cannot just reassign points because we don't have per-point data. The problem likely expects that we only consider clusters that have size >= min_cluster_size, and we compute stability only for those. The parent relationship among retained clusters must be derived from the original parent mapping, but if a cluster is dropped, its children become children of its parent (if that parent is retained) or further up. However, the input parent mapping includes all clusters, so we need to build a condensed tree where only clusters with size >= min_cluster_size are nodes, and then the parent-child relationships are defined by the original hierarchy after removing the small clusters. For each retained cluster, its parent is the nearest retained ancestor (which could be the original parent if retained, or grandparent, etc). Also, points that belong to a dropped cluster are essentially absorbed into the parent retained cluster. But then the stability for the parent should include the exit lambdas of those points? However the parent's point_exit_lambdas do not include those from dropped children. So the stability should be computed only from the points directly assigned to the cluster? That seems inconsistent. Let's re-read the problem statement: \"For each retained cluster sum max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) over its points.\" It says \"its points\" - which points? The points that are in that cluster after condensation? Typically in HDBSCAN, after removing small clusters, the points that were in those small clusters are said to \"fall out\" at the lambda of the small cluster's birth, i.e., they exit the parent cluster at that lambda. But here the input provides explicit point_exit_lambdas per cluster. That suggests that the clusters are already defined with their point exit lambdas, and we are to condense based on size. So when we drop a small cluster, its points are not considered in any cluster's stability. But that would mean the total stability is not conserved. Alternatively, maybe the point_exit_lambdas are for the points that are \"owned\" by the cluster at its birth, and after condensation, those points get reassigned to the nearest ancestor cluster that is retained, and their exit lambdas remain the same. However the parent cluster's point_exit_lambdas list might not contain those points. But the problem says \"Use only quantities present in the case payload.\" So we cannot create new points. Therefore, it's more plausible that we treat each cluster independently: the stability is computed from its own point_exit_lambdas, regardless of dropping other clusters. But then the selection logic: we select clusters based on stability, but we must ensure that no selected cluster has a descendant also selected. That's a standard independent selection. And dropping small clusters means they are not considered for selection at all. And the parent relationship among retained clusters is determined by the original parent mapping, treating dropped clusters as if they don't exist (so a retained child's parent becomes the nearest retained ancestor). This is typical for condensed tree. The cluster's points are still the original ones, but the cluster may lose points to descendants? Actually in HDBSCAN, the condensed tree does not include points directly; it only has clusters with birth lambda and lambda at which each point becomes part of a child cluster (or noise). The point_exit_lambdas for a cluster are the lambdas at which points leave that cluster (i.e., become part of a child cluster or become noise). The points that leave to form a child cluster are not counted in the parent's stability? Wait, stability: For a cluster, we sum over all points that are in the cluster at its birth, of (exit_lambda - birth_lambda), but only for points that have exit lambda > birth lambda. Points that leave to form a child cluster have exit lambda = child's birth lambda? Actually in HDBSCAN, the cluster's stability includes all points that are in the cluster at its birth, and the exit lambda for a point is the lambda at which it leaves the cluster (i.e., the lambda of the next cluster it joins, or infinity if it stays). For points that remain in the cluster until the end, exit lambda = infinity, so stability contribution is infinite? But the problem's example doesn't include infinity. Alternatively, typical implementation: exit_lambda is the lambda at which the point becomes noise or leaves the cluster. For points that are inherited by a child cluster, the child cluster's birth lambda is that exit lambda for the parent. So the parent's stability excludes the points that go to children? No, the parent's stability includes all points at its birth, and each point's contribution is (exit_lambda - birth_lambda) if exit_lambda > birth_lambda. Points that go to children have exit_lambda = child's birth_lambda, which is > parent's birth_lambda, so they contribute to parent's stability. Points that become noise have exit_lambda = that noise lambda. So stability is sum over all points in the cluster at its birth. In the condensed tree, each point belongs to exactly one cluster at any point? Actually each point is in the root cluster at its birth (lambda=0) and then moves down to child clusters as lambda increases. The stability of a cluster is the total persistence of points within that cluster. For the purpose of selection, we want to select clusters that maximize stability. The algorithm: start from leaves, compute stability for each cluster, then propagate upwards? Typically, we compute the \"stability\" for each cluster as sum over points of (exit_lambda - birth_lambda), but then for a cluster, we also consider the stability of its descendants. The selection algorithm: for each cluster, compute the total stability of the subtree rooted at that cluster (including the cluster's own stability and the sum of stabilities of selected clusters in its subtree). Then compare the cluster's own stability vs the sum of stabilities of its children (if children are selected). The typical HDBSCAN algorithm selects a cluster if its own stability is greater than the sum of stabilities of its children (or if it has no children). But the problem statement says: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" That is a different approach: It processes clusters in decreasing stability order, and for each cluster, if it is not a descendant of an already selected cluster, and it has no selected descendant, then select it, and then block its ancestors (i.e., no ancestor can be selected later). That is simpler. Given the problem's description, we should implement that.\n\nSo steps:\n\n1. Validate input: Ensure min_cluster_size is int or number, clusters list has required fields, parent mapping covers all cluster_ids, etc. Raise ValueError if invalid.\n\n2. Filter clusters: keep only those with size >= min_cluster_size. For retained clusters, compute stability = sum(max(0, exit - birth_lambda) for exit in point_exit_lambdas).\n\n3. Build a mapping of retained cluster_id to its stability and size (maybe needed for tie-breaking? Tie-breaking uses cluster_id, so we need to sort by (-stability, cluster_id) where cluster_id is string).\n\n4. Build a new parent mapping for retained clusters: For each retained cluster, find its nearest retained ancestor by traversing original parent chain until we hit a retained cluster or null. If the root (parent null) is not retained (but root must be retained because its size is >= min_cluster_size? Not necessarily: if root cluster size < min_cluster_size, then there is no root; but the problem's example has root size 7 >=3, so it's fine. If root is not retained, then some clusters may have no retained ancestor; then they become de facto roots? But typical condensed tree: if all clusters are dropped, there is no tree. However we need to handle: If a retained cluster has no retained ancestor (i.e., all ancestors dropped including root), then its parent is None. That's allowed.\n\n5. For selecting clusters: We need to order retained clusters by decreasing stability, then by cluster_id (presumably ascending). We'll sort list of (stability, cluster_id) tuples: sort by (-stability, cluster_id). The cluster_id is a string, so compare lexicographically.\n\n6. Then we need to know for each cluster its ancestors and descendants. Since clusters form a tree (or forest) after condensation, we can build parent-child relationships from the new parent mapping. Then we can compute for each cluster its set of descendants (maybe only needed to check if any selected descendant exists) and ancestors (to block). We'll do a topological traversal: first compute children lists.\n\n7. Selection algorithm:\n   - Initialize selected = set().\n   - Process clusters in sorted order:\n        Check if any descendant of this cluster is already selected. If yes, skip (blocked by descendant).\n        Check if any ancestor of this cluster is already selected. If yes, skip (blocked by ancestor). Actually the problem says \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" So we only check descendants: if no selected descendant, then select it, and then block its ancestors (i.e., mark ancestors as blocked). But note: later when we process an ancestor, it will see that a descendant is selected (the one we just selected), so it will be blocked. But also if we process a cluster and its ancestor is already selected, that ancestor would have blocked it? But according to the algorithm: we process in decreasing stability, so if an ancestor has higher stability, it would be processed first. If ancestor is selected, then its descendants are blocked because they have a selected ancestor. But the algorithm as stated only checks descendants not ancestors. Actually typical HDBSCAN selection: process clusters in decreasing stability; for each, if it is not a descendant of any selected cluster, then consider its own stability vs children's stability. But the problem's description is different. Let's read: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" That sounds like: when considering a cluster, look at its descendants. If any descendant has already been selected, then do not select this cluster (because descendant blocks it). Otherwise, select it, and then block its ancestors (meaning that ancestors cannot be selected later because they have a selected descendant). So we need to check only descendants. But what about ancestors that are already selected? If an ancestor is already selected, then this cluster is a descendant of that selected cluster, so it would have been blocked? But the selection of the ancestor would have blocked the descendant? The problem's wording \"when no already selected descendant blocks it\" implies we only care about descendants, not ancestors. So if an ancestor is selected, we ignore that? That would allow selecting a cluster that is a descendant of an already selected cluster. But typical HDBSCAN forbids that because a selected cluster is supposed to be a maximal stability cluster; selecting a descendant of an already selected cluster would be inconsistent because the descendant's points are a subset of the ancestor's points. However the algorithm given might be: process in decreasing stability; for a cluster, if it has any selected descendant, skip; else select it and then mark all ancestors as blocked (so they won't be selected later). In that case, when we later process an ancestor, it will see that its descendant is selected, so it will be blocked. And if an ancestor was processed earlier and selected, then its descendants will later be processed and they will see that the ancestor (which is not a descendant but an ancestor) is selected; but the condition only checks descendants, not ancestors. So a descendant could be selected even if its ancestor was already selected. That would be wrong. So I think the intended algorithm is: process in decreasing stability; for each cluster, if it is not a descendant of any already selected cluster (i.e., no ancestor that is selected), then consider it; then if its own stability is greater than the sum of stabilities of its children (or something), select it and block its ancestors. But the problem explicitly says \"no already selected descendant blocks it\", not \"ancestor\". Let's trust the problem statement. Possibly they mean \"no already selected cluster that is a descendant of it blocks it\"? That is, no selected cluster that is a descendant of the current cluster? That would be the usual: a cluster can only be selected if none of its descendants are selected (because those would have higher stability? Actually in HDBSCAN, we select a cluster if its own stability is greater than the total stability of its children; so if a child is selected, the parent is not selected because the child's stability is part of the sum. But the selection order is from leaves up, so children are processed before parents. So \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" means if any descendant is already selected, the parent cannot be selected because the descendant's stability is already part of the subtree. Then after selecting the parent, we block its ancestors (so they cannot be selected later). So that matches: we process clusters in decreasing stability (which is not necessarily leaf-first; but leaves have lower stability? Usually leaves have lower stability). However the problem says \"Rank clusters by decreasing stability and cluster ID.\" So we process from highest stability to lowest. Leaves may have lower stability than their parent. So a leaf with low stability might be processed after its parent (if parent has higher stability). In that case, when processing the parent, there is no selected descendant yet, so parent gets selected, then blocks its ancestors. Later when processing the leaf (descendant), it sees that its ancestor (parent) is selected, but the condition only checks descendants, not ancestors. So the leaf would not be blocked by its ancestor being selected. That would allow selecting both parent and leaf, which is incorrect. So the condition must also check ancestors. Let's re-read: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" It doesn't say anything about ancestors being already selected. So if an ancestor was selected earlier, the cluster might still be eligible because it has no selected descendant (since the selected ancestor is not its descendant). Then it would be selected, and then block its ancestors. That could lead to two selected clusters where one is ancestor of the other. That defeats the purpose. Therefore, I suspect a mis-statement. Possibly it means: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected cluster that is an ancestor of it blocks it\" but then \"block its ancestors\" would be redundant. Alternatively, it might mean: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, otherwise block its ancestors.\" But that doesn't make sense.\n\nLet's look at typical HDBSCAN selection: After computing stabilities, we compute for each cluster the \"excess of mass\" compared to its children. Usually we propagate from leaves upwards, and for each cluster we compare its own stability to the sum of stabilities of its children. If its own stability is greater, we select it and exclude children; otherwise we keep children. That is a bottom-up approach. But the problem describes a top-down selection with blocking. Many implementations do the selection as described in the original HDBSCAN paper: While there is a cluster with stability > sum of stabilities of its child clusters, select that cluster and exclude its children. This is iterative and uses a priority queue sorted by stability.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I'll go with a common interpretation: Process clusters in decreasing stability. For each cluster, if none of its descendants have been selected, and it is not a descendant of any selected cluster (i.e., no ancestor selected), then select it, and then mark its ancestors as blocked (so they cannot be selected later). That ensures no cluster is selected that has a selected ancestor or descendant. But the problem's wording only says \"no already selected descendant blocks it\". To be safe, I'll implement the condition as: \"if the cluster has no selected descendants and it is not a descendant of any selected cluster\" (i.e., no selected ancestor). But the problem says \"then block its ancestors\". That indicates after selecting, we block ancestors. So we need to store for each cluster whether it is blocked. When selecting a cluster, we block all its ancestors (mark them as blocked). Then for subsequent clusters, if they are blocked, skip. Also we need to check if any selected descendant exists. But if we block ancestors, then any descendant of a selected cluster will have a blocked ancestor (the selected cluster itself or higher). But the condition checks descending, not upward. So we need both checks: if not blocked and no selected descendant, then select, then block ancestors.\n\nSimplify: Let blocked[cluster] = False initially. Then for each cluster in sorted order:\n    if blocked[cluster] or any(descendant in selected): but we need efficient check. Perhaps we can compute for each cluster, the set of descendants. But that's heavy. Alternative: Process in decreasing stability, and maintain a set of selected clusters. For each cluster, if any selected cluster is an ancestor or descendant, skip. But descendant check is expensive. Better to propagate blocking upward after selection.\n\nWe'll implement as follows:\n- Build tree of retained clusters: each node has parent (or None) and list of children.\n- Compute ancestor sets and descendant sets (maybe using BFS from each node? Could be O(n^2) but n small in test? Not specified, but can be up to reasonable size. We'll precompute for each cluster its descendant set by DFS.\n- Then iterate sorted clusters:\n    if any descendant (in descendant set) is selected: skip.\n    if any ancestor is selected: skip? According to \"no already selected descendant blocks it\", we only check descendants. But to avoid descendant being selected after ancestor, we can also check ancestors? Actually if we only check descendants, then an ancestor selected earlier would not block a descendant. But the descendant might later be selected, causing two selected with ancestral relationship. That seems wrong. So I'll also check ancestors: if any ancestor selected, skip (this ensures no cluster is selected if any of its ancestors are selected). However the problem says \"then block its ancestors\" which means after selection, we block ancestors so they cannot be selected later. So we can combine: after selecting a cluster, mark all ancestors as blocked. Then later, when we encounter an ancestor, it will be blocked and skipped. So we don't need ancestor check in the condition, because if an ancestor was selected earlier, that ancestor would have blocked all its ancestors, but the descendant would not be blocked because it's not an ancestor. But the descendant would still be considered; and if it has no selected descendant, it could be selected, leading to both selected. So we need to also check if the cluster is a descendant of a selected cluster. So either we check ancestors in condition or we propagate a \"blocked\" flag downwards as well. Common approach: When selecting a cluster, block its ancestors, and also mark its descendants as \"ineligible\" because they cannot be selected (since they are contained in selected cluster). But the problem doesn't say that. It says \"block its ancestors\". So only ancestors are blocked, not descendants. That implies that after selecting a cluster, its descendants are still eligible? That can't be. A selected cluster already contains its descendants' points. Selecting a descendant would double-count. So maybe the intent is that the selection algorithm is: process clusters in decreasing stability; if a cluster is not contained within any already selected cluster (i.e., it is not a descendant of a selected cluster) and it has no selected descendant, then select it. Then block its ancestors (so they cannot be selected later). This ensures no overlap. I'll adopt that: condition: not (cluster's ancestor in selected) AND not (cluster's descendant in selected). Then select, then block ancestors. This matches typical max-stability selection.\n\nGiven the problem's phrasing \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" might be a simplification meaning \"it is not the ancestor of a selected cluster\", i.e., it has no selected descendant. But they didn't mention the ancestor condition. However, typical HDBSCAN selection from leaves up naturally prevents ancestors from being selected after children. But here we sort by decreasing stability, which could be top-down. So we must include both.\n\nI'll implement based on the standard algorithm: For each cluster in decreasing stability, if it has no selected descendant and it is not a descendant of any selected cluster, then select it and block its ancestors. After processing all, the selected set is the final clusters. Then return stability rows for all retained clusters (even those not selected?) The problem says \"Return all retained stability rows and sorted selected IDs.\" So output should include all retained clusters with their stability, and a list of selected cluster IDs sorted (presumably in ascending order of cluster_id? Or by stability? It says \"sorted selected IDs\", likely sorted ascending by cluster ID string.\n\nNow output format: It must be a JSON-serializable object. The function returns that. I'll define a dict with keys: \"clusters\" (list of dicts for each retained cluster with cluster_id, stability, size?) or just stability? The problem says \"Return all retained stability rows\". Could be a list of objects with cluster_id and stability. Also \"sorted selected IDs\" as a list of strings. I'll return {\"stability\": {cluster_id: stability}, \"selected\": [list sorted]}. But need exact named fields? The problem says \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields.\" It doesn't specify the exact structure beyond \"all retained stability rows and sorted selected IDs\". Possibly it expects an object with keys \"clusters\" and \"selected\". I'll look at the fixture: it doesn't show expected output. But typical tasks like this expect something like: {\"clusters\": [{\"cluster_id\": ..., \"stability\": ...}, ...], \"selected\": [\"c0\", ...]}. I'll go with that.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- Define solve(case) that extracts payload from case (case is a dict with keys case_id, payload).\n- Validate payload: must have min_cluster_size, clusters (list), parent (dict). Check types: min_cluster_size should be int/float, clusters each dict with cluster_id (string), size (int), birth_lambda (float/int), point_exit_lambdas (list of floats/ints). parent dict mapping string to string or None. Validate that all cluster_ids in parent keys are in clusters, and parent values are either None or exist in clusters. Also ensure point_exit_lambdas length equals size. Raise ValueError if any violation.\n\n- Filter clusters: new list retained = [c for c in clusters if c['size'] >= min_cluster_size]. If no retained clusters, then selected is empty, but we still need to return something? The problem might consider that valid. We'll handle.\n\n- Compute stability for each retained: for each cluster, sum(max(0, exit - birth_lambda) for exit in point_exit_lambdas). Store in dict stability_map.\n\n- Build parent map for retained: original_parent map. For each retained cluster, find its nearest retained ancestor:\n    def find_parent(cluster_id):\n        p = original_parent[cluster_id]\n        while p is not None and p not in retained_ids:\n            p = original_parent[p]\n        return p  # could be None\n    Build children lists: for each retained, if its parent is in retained, add child to parent's children. Also store map from id to node object (store parent, children, stability, etc.)\n\n- Compute descendant sets: can use recursion or iterative topological order. Since tree is small, we can compute for each node a set of descendants (including itself? we'll exclude itself for check). We'll do DFS from root(s) and compute descendants.\n\n- Sort retained clusters by ( -stability, cluster_id ). Create list of tuples.\n\n- Initialize selected = set(), blocked = set() (ancestors blocked). But we'll also store ancestor sets for each node to quickly check if any ancest